WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.792
The default should be “not yet”.

2
00:00:02.072 --> 00:00:07.064
Lesson 1 gave you four questions and lesson 2 gave you five workflow patterns.

3
00:00:07.344 --> 00:00:18.885
This lesson makes the case for the conservative answer with numbers, because “agents are less reliable” is easy to say and easy to ignore until you see how fast the arithmetic turns against you.

4
00:00:19.165 --> 00:00:31.837
The Anthropic post that framed this week is explicit that agents bring higher costs and the potential for compounding errors, and recommends extensive testing in sandboxed environments with guardrails.

5
00:00:32.117 --> 00:00:43.317
Its broader advice is that many applications never need an agentic system at all, and that optimizing a single call with retrieval and in-context examples is usually enough.

6
00:00:43.597 --> 00:00:53.005
Barry Zhang’s talk, below, puts the same idea as a working rule: build agents for complex, valuable tasks where errors can be caught, and nowhere else.

7
00:00:53.285 --> 00:00:56.165
None of this is an argument that agents don’t work.

8
00:00:56.445 --> 00:01:02.675
Coding agents are useful today, and the measurements later in this lesson show capability growing fast.

9
00:01:02.955 --> 00:01:12.683
It is an argument about fit: for a given task, an agent is one option with a particular cost and failure profile, and you should choose it when the numbers favor it.
